  {
   "id": 54,
   "type": "Note",
   "pos": [
    60,
    -420
   ],
   "size": [
    500,
    380
   ],
   "flags": {},
   "order": 0,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "FAST DE-ROPE + ANIME CADENCE\n\nSame smear fix as the 2-pass MAINodes de-rope, with the extra cost roughly halved (Re:Zero i2v 1024x576x124 on a 5090: 246 s -> 167 s; a plain render is ~100 s).\nWhy H3 smears: the latent CAN hold crisp fast poses (VAE round trip is clean); the DiT paints blur when a latent token has to carry fast motion inside it. The de-rope's holds give each fast pose its own static token, but the slowed clip is 2-3x longer and full attention grows with the square of its length.\nH3 Window Attention: in pass 2 each frame attends exactly to its neighbours (+-4 latent frames), every 8th frame as an anchor, and all text/audio/first-frame rows. Pass 2 ~40% faster; fast poses came out as crisp or crisper than dense attention in our tests. window 2 is faster but starts inventing effects.\ntaeh3 does the throwaway decode/encode of the slowed clip (34 s -> 2 s); the int8 video VAE does the final decode.\nAudio is pass 1's normal soundtrack (Exact Recover keeps pass 1's timing)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 55,
   "type": "Note",
   "pos": [
    580,
    -420
   ],
   "size": [
    500,
    380
   ],
   "flags": {},
   "order": 0,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "DRAWING DE-ROPE (2s)\n\nInside the gated burst, anime only needs a new drawing every 2 frames, so only those drawings are slowed down and redrawn: each gets one static latent token, aligned to H3's token grid (key poses such as impacts get two, so pass 2 commits to them; shot cuts always start a new drawing). Drawing Recover holds each redrawn drawing over its 2-frame slot: the burst comes back crisp and already on 2s. Pass 2 was 8-26% cheaper than the per-frame fast de-rope in our tests (more on long bursts), same crispness, impacts on the same frames."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 56,
   "type": "Note",
   "pos": [
    1100,
    -420
   ],
   "size": [
    500,
    380
   ],
   "flags": {},
   "order": 0,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "WIDE-SHOT FACE FIX\n\nWhy wide-shot faces go mushy in H3: the DiT draws in 32x32 px tokens (16x VAE + 2x2 patch). A 35 px face in a 1024x576 wide shot is ~2 tokens, so eyes and mouth have nowhere to go. Higher output resolution barely helps (the face is still a tiny share of the frame).\nH3 Face Atlas finds every anime face (ONNX detector, CPU), tracks it per shot, and magnifies each one into its own 512 px tile of ONE canvas: ~60 tokens per face instead of ~2, all characters in a single pass. The whole frame sits above the tiles at half size, pinned (not redrawn), so the faces are redrawn while seeing the scene: no stray effects pasted onto faces and no extra boiling. Pass 1's audio is pinned, so mouths follow the dialogue.\nUse the SAME prompt + style LoRA as the render. Faces already big on screen are skipped (close-ups pass through untouched)."
   ]
  }
